  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nCurrent regional radar imagery highlights an area of isolated to \nscattered showers approaching from the west. These showers are \nassociated with a shortwave trough that will move across the Ohio & \nTennessee valleys over the next 24 hours. The low level jet will \nbegin to ramp up ahead of the approaching system overnight into \nSunday. This will induce broad isentropic lift over middle TN and \nacross the Plateau counties. There remains some uncertainty with how \nfar east the precipitation will push on Sunday as residual dry air \nwill limit rain due to evaporation. The highest confidence of any \nrainfall is west of interstate 75, but it wouldn't surprise me if \nsome sprinkles or brief showers make it east of the interstate. \nGusty winds will be possible overnight into Sunday as a weak \nmountain wave event unfolds across the mountains and foothills. Wind \nspeeds and gusts will remain below advisory criteria. \n\nThe first week of November will be a bit of a roller coaster. Monday \nand Tuesday feature a return to the mild and dry pattern we've \nobserved over the past few weeks. The broad surface high pressure \nover the eastern CONUS and west Atlantic will build back into the \nregion. Aloft, we'll see upper level ridging return and hold steady \nthrough Tuesday. High temperatures will be in the mid to upper 70s \nand lows in the 50s. The signs of a pattern change are present \nupstream. An atmospheric river event is unfolding over the SW US and \nthe associated upper trough will slowly work it's way east into the \nPlains early week. Ensemble guidance features some spread with \nrespect to the intensity, track, and timing of the mid-week system \nand frontal passage. However, there is strong consensus with this \nupper trough bringing the Tennessee valley meaningful rain at some \npoint this week. Our upper level trough will cross the Rockies \nSunday night into Monday. Our associated surface low forms over \nColorado Sunday night and treks northeast to the Upper Mississippi \nvalley by Tuesday night. From there guidance spread increases \nsignificantly, but the front will move very slowly from middle TN to \neastern TN Wednesday afternoon into Thursday. This front will bring \nmuch needed rain to the area given how dry it has been this Fall. \nEnsemble probabilities are support a pretty strong signal for at \nleast a quarter inch (0.25\") or more of rain across the entire CWA. \nThe best signal for higher amounts is over our western CWA at this \ntime. Our system will also bring another potential mountain wave \ndownslope wind event for the mountains and foothills. \n\nFor Friday into next weekend, our temperatures will cool down to \nmore seasonable values with highs in the upper 60s and lows back in \nthe 40s. Next weekend looks dry, but things could change given the \nuncertainty in timing with the mid week system.",
